Abstract
The combination of noble metal nanoparticles (NM NPs) and metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for enhanced properties in catalysis is a rapidly developing interdisciplinary research field. Herein we report a facile one-step approach to the deposition of functional nanocomposite shell composed of NM NPs and conducting polymer on the surface of particulate UiO-66. Our approach is based on the recently developed double solvents method involving a hydrophobic solvent ( $n$ -hexane) and a hydrophilic solvent (water). Particularly, the $n$ -hexane was used in a large amount to suspend the UiO-66 particles with hydrophilic inner pores and promoted the enrichment of the water containing the palladium chloride $(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2})$ inside the pores of UiO-66 particles, because these two solvents are immiscible and the volume of water is smaller than the pore volume of the UiO-66 particles. After the introduction of hydrophobic pyrrole into the nhexane, the UiO-66 particles are successfully coated with a nanocomposite shell, composed of Pd NPs and polypyrrole (PPy) resulting from the interfacial polymerization of pyrrole on the external surface of UiO-66 particles initiated by $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ presented within the UiO-66 particles. Significantly, in such a unique structure, the prepared hybrid particles noted as UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles are demonstrated to be an excellent catalyst with both high activity and stability in the reduction reaction of $p$ -nitrophenol and sodium borohydride. Furthermore, this synthetic method not only effectively controls the mass loading of Pd NPs and PPy on the UiO66 particles but also enables the deposition of Au $@$ PPy and $\operatorname{Ag}@\operatorname{PPy}$ nanocomposite shells onto the surface of the UiO-66 particles.
Keywords: UiO-66, palladium nanoparticle, polypyrrole, nanocomposite particle, catalysis
## 1. Introduction
Noble metal nanoparticles (NM NPs) have attracted great interest in catalysis due to their high surface-to-volume ratio and thus a large number of edge and corner atoms.[1-4] Yet, their small size also brings several drawbacks, such as fast deactivation and being difficult to be separated and recycled, because they are thermodynamically unstable and tend to aggregate into clusters during practical uses.[5, 6] Therefore, many endeavors have been devoted to developing effective methods to overcome these shortcomings. Typically, these NM NPs are supported on the surface of solids (such as reduced graphene oxide, porous carbon, polystyrene latex, etc.) with high surface area as supported catalysts in various catalytic reactions.[7-10]
Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed from metal ions interconnected by rigid organic linkers, have been an ideal candidate for supporting catalytically active NM NPs on their surface. The MOFs are usually highly porous with high surface area, diverse chemical compositions, exceptional structural stability, and some unique interactions between MOFs and NM NPs have been reported.[11-13] For example, gold-palladium (Au-Pd) alloy NPs and platinum $\mathrm{\bf(Pt)}$ NPs have been assembled on the surface of different MOFs, and the resultant supported NM NPs were successfully applied to selective oxidation of saturated hydrocarbons and boosting photocatalytic hydrogen production, respectively.[14, 15] However, the main challenge is the possibility of sintering of NM NPs during catalytic reactions at harsh conditions such as high temperature. Moreover, the detachment of NM NPs from the support surface is also an important issue that needs to be addressed. Hence, the combination of NM NPs and MOFs for new or enhanced properties is still a rapidly developing interdisciplinary research field.
Recently, it has been realized that depositing an additional protective layer on top of the supported NM NPs is an effective approach to address the problems of particle sintering and detaching from the support. More significantly, the protective layer additionally allows the selective diffusion of reaction substrates to be close to catalytic sites owing to their molecular size and wettability, thus enabling the enhancement of catalytic activity and selectivity.[16-19] For example, Jiang et al. reported the surface hydrophobicity of UiO-66 supported palladium (Pd) NPs with a simple polydimethylsiloxane coating can enhance the catalytic activity of Pd NPs in reactions involving hydrophobic reactants and simultaneously afforded the superior catalytic recyclability.[16] Tang and co-workers demonstrated a sandwich-like structure of
MOF@Pt@MOF that represented a stable catalyst with high activity and with remarkably enhanced selectivity for converting a range of α, $\upbeta$ -unsaturated aldehydes towards α, $\upbeta$ - unsaturated alcohols.[17]
On the other hand, recent studies showed that a combination of conducting polymers (CPs) and NM NPs leads to the improved catalytic performance because of the synergetic properties belonging to these two components.[20] Inspired by these pioneering works, it is expected that CPs can be an ideal protective layer used for the NM NPs supported on the MOFs. However, no advance has been reported so far on three-component nanocomposite particles prepared by coating a MOF with CPs and NM NPs simultaneously, although there have been some studies on the deposition of MOFs with either NM NPs or CPs.[14, 21-23]
In this work, we propose a facile one-pot approach to control synthesis of the particulate MOFs covered by nanocomposite overlayers comprising of both NM NPs and CPs. We apply double solvents method developed by Xu et al. to introduce $\mathrm{\PdCl}_{2}$ aqueous solution into internal hydrophilic pores of UiO-66 particles and hydrophobic pyrrole monomer into hexane, respectively.[24-26] Then, the interfacial polymerization of pyrrole is initiated by $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ to form nanocomposite shell composed of Pd NPs and polypyrrole (PPy) simultaneously on the external surface of UiO-66 particles. Our results show that the as-prepared UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles display high activity and stability in the catalytic reduction of $p$ - nitrophenol (4-NP) by sodium borohydride $\mathrm{(NaBH_{4})}$ ). Moreover, our preparation method can be easily extended to the deposition of PPy embedded with other NM NPs, such as Au NPs and silver $(\mathbf{Ag})$ NPs, on the UiO-66.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials
All chemicals were commercial and used as received without further purification. $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ , 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid ( $\mathrm{\bf~\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC})$ were purchased from Energy Chemical. N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{\HAuCl_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{\bfAgNO}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ , methanol, $p$ - nitrophenol (4-NP), acetic acid and $n$ -hexane were obtained from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co.. Pyrrole was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Ultrapure water $\cdot18.2~\mathrm{M}\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm})$ was used throughout the experiments.
## 2.2 Synthesis of UiO-66 particles
In a typical procedure, $400~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , $534~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ and $11~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetic acid were added to $160~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF under stirring. After $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ , the above-mentioned mixture was kept at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resulting UiO-66 particles were collected by centrifugation and washed three times with DMF. The product was then immersed in methanol for three days with replacing solvent every $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . At last, the UiO-66 particles were activated overnight under vacuum at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.3 Synthesis of UiO-66/Pd@PPy nanocomposite particles
Typically, $60~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of UiO-66 particles were first suspended in 15 mL of $n$ -hexane under ultrasonication. After that, $15~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ aqueous solution $(0.5{\bf M})$ was added dropwise over a period of $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ under vigorous stirring and the resulting mixture was stirred for another $3\mathrm{~h~}$ . At last, $15\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of pyrrole was injected dropwise to the above mixture and the reaction was allowed to proceed for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resultant UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles were collected and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
2.4 Synthesis of UiO-66/Au@PPy and UiO-66/Ag@PPy nanocomposite particles
The procedures for the synthesis of UiO-66/Au@PPy and UiO-66/Ag@PPy nanocomposite particles were similar to that of UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles, except for replacing $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ with $\mathrm{\HAuCl_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}.$ , respectively.
## 2.5 Catalytic experiments
$120~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 4-NP (2.5 mM), $2.52\mathrm{mL}$ of water, and a given amount of the aqueous dispersion of catalyst particles were added into a quartz cuvette. Then, $300~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of freshly prepared $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ (0.1 M) were injected to the cuvette to initiate the reaction. UV-vis spectroscopy was used to monitor the reduction of 4-NP to 4-AP. Finally, the catalyst particles were recovered from the reaction mixture by centrifugation and reused in the next cycle.
## 2.6 Characterization
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) measurements were carried out with an S-4800 instrument (Hitachi Co., Japan). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) measurements were performed using a JEM-2100 microscope (JEOL Co., Japan). Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy was conducted on a Nicolet 6700 spectrometer in the scanning range of 4000–500 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES, Agilent, 700 Series) was used to analyze the metal content in the nanocomposite. X-ray diffraction (XRD) analyses were performed on a German Bruker-AXS D8 Advance. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was recorded on a VG ESCALABMKII $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray photoelectron spectrometer. N2 adsorption and desorption isotherm measurements were performed on ASAP $2020~\mathrm{MP}$ at 77K. Samples were activated under vacuum at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\textrm{h}$ . UV-vis absorption spectra were collected on a SHIMAZDU UV-2700 spectrometer at room temperature.
## 3. Results and discussion
Scheme 1. Preparation process of UiO-66/Pd@PPy nanocomposite particles.
In our work, a typical MOF (UiO-66) particle was selected as support due to its facile synthesis, high stability, and large specific surface area.[27, 28] Scheme 1 illustrates the preparation process of UiO-66/Pd@PPy nanocomposite particles prepared by the double solvents method. The as-prepared UiO-66 particles were first dispersed in a large amount of hexane under ultrasonic treatment, followed by the addition of $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ aqueous solution with a volume smaller than the pore volume of UiO-66. Benefiting from the hydrophilic inner surface of UiO-66 particles as well as the overspreading of their external surface by the hydrophobic hexane, the added $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ aqueous solution could diffuse into the hydrophilic pores of UiO-66 by capillary action. After the introduction of hydrophobic pyrrole into hexane, it could encounter the $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ aqueous solution at the interface between pore windows of UiO-66 particles and hexane. It then would initiate interfacial reaction, i.e., chemical oxidation polymerization of pyrrole monomer, in which the $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ played dual roles of an oxidant for pyrrole and a source of the Pd NPs, ultimately leading to the formation of UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles.
The SEM images of UiO-66 particles and as-prepared UiO-66/Pd@PPy nanocomposite particles are shown in Fig. 1. A typical octahedral morphology of UiO-66 particles with relatively uniform size distribution can be clearly seen in Fig. 1a. As for UiO-66/Pd@PPy nanocomposite particles, a representative SEM image gives a direct evidence for the presence of polymer shell on the external surface of each UiO-66 particle. The morphology of resultant nanocomposite particles becomes slightly irregular, simultaneously resulting in a larger particle size, as shown in Fig. 1c. Further characterization using TEM indicates the Pd NPs with an average size of about $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ are well encapsulated in the PPy shell without appreciable aggregation. The Pd loading in the prepared nanocomposite particles was $1.1~\mathrm{wt}\%$ , as determined by ICP-OES.
In addition, the presence of PPy on the UiO-66 particles can be verified by FTIR spectroscopy (Fig. 2). In comparison with the UiO-66 particles, several addition absorption bands notably at about 1226, 1151, and $929\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , corresponding to the PPy component, can be clearly observed in the FTIR spectrum of prepared nanocomposite particles.[22] This can be regarded as supporting evidence for successful deposition of PPy shell on the UiO-66 particles. In our system, the composition and morphology of UiO-66/Pd@PPy can be easily controlled by changing the amount of $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ aqueous solution (Fig. S1-S3). The increment in the amount of pyrrole has increased the Pd loading (e.g., $2.2\ \mathrm{{and}}\ 4.0\ \mathrm{{wt\%}})$ and the content of PPy in the resultant nanocomposite particles. However, the aggregation of nanocomposite particles occurred when the volume of $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ aqueous solution was larger than the pore volume of UiO-66 particles. We proposed that, the portion of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ cations tend to react with pyrrole outside the UiO-66 particles and the precipitation of Pd@PPy nanocomposites could be randomly and simultaneously adsorbed by different UiO-66 particles.
The structure and chemical composition of as-prepared nanocomposite particles was further verified by XRD, XPS, and ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption analysis. The XRD patterns of UiO-66 particles and UiO-66/Pd@PPy nanocomposite particles are displayed in Fig. 3. It can be seen that pristine UiO-66 particles fit well with that given in the previous reports, demonstrating the successful synthesis of UiO-66.[29, 30] Moreover, the agreement of the diffraction peaks between these two samples clearly indicates the preservation of high crystallinity of UiO-66 particles after the formation of UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles. Although the signals of Pd component in the nanocomposite particles was hard to be directly detected, there is an appreciable broad diffraction peak at about $2\theta=40.1^{\circ}$ in an enlarged region located from 35 to $45^{\mathrm{~o~}}$ (inset), which can be indexed to the (111) crystalline plane of $\mathrm{Pd}(0)$ . This result suggested the presence of Pd NPs with small size and low loading in the PPy shell, consistent with the TEM and ICP-OES analyses.
Furthermore, Fig. 4a shows the XPS survey spectrum of the as-prepared nanocomposite particles. It can be clearly seen that the main peaks belong to C 1s, O 1s, Zr 3p, Zr 3d, N 1s, Cl 2p and Pd 3d. Fig. 4b depicts the core spectrum of Pd 3d, which exhibit two peaks centered at about 336.6 and $341.9\mathrm{eV},$ respectively, corresponding to characteristic for $\operatorname{Pd}(0)$ species. Hence, these phenomena demonstrate the presence of $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{PPy}$ nanocomposite shell on the UiO-66 particles. However, the formation of nanocomposite shell would not severely affect the structure and porosity of UiO-66 support, which were revealed from ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption and desorption isotherms of UiO-66/Pd@PPy nanocomposite particles (Fig. 5). The BET surface area and pore volume of UiO-66 particle were determined to be about $1052~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ and $0.609\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Fig. S4). The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm of nanocomposite particles exhibited similar shape but the BET surface area was decreased to some extent compared with the intrinsic UiO66 particles.[16, 18, 31] However, the retentive high surface area $(600~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and pore volume (0.369 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{~\}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ of UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles suggested the Pd@PPy nanocomposite shell was permeable, which could permit the transportation of small molecules.
Significantly, our facile synthetic strategy reported here is also general and can be extended to the synthesis of $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{PPy}$ and $\operatorname{Ag}@\operatorname{PPy}$ nanocomposite particles supported on the UiO-66 particles, when $\mathrm{\HAuCl_{4}}$ and ${\bf A g N O}_{3}$ are used instead of $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ . As represented in Fig. 6, it can be clearly seen in SEM and TEM images that the uniform PPy layer with small NPs were well coated on the UiO-66 particles. In addition, the FTIR spectra and XRD patterns also show that the existence of PPy component and corresponding noble metal NPs in such nanocomposite particles (Fig. S5-S6).
In order to verify the effectiveness of as-prepared UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles as heterogeneous catalyst, their catalytic performance was evaluated in the reduction of 4-NP by $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ , which is widely used for examining the catalytic performance of various noble metal-based nanocatalysts.[32-34] In Fig. 7a, the 4-NP aqueous solution shows a maximum absorption at about 317 nm. However, upon the addition of $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ , the absorption peak appears at about $400~\mathrm{nm}$ , along with the colour change of solution from pale yellow to bright yellow, owing to the formation of nitrophenolate ion under alkaline condition. In the absence of a catalyst, there was negligible change in the adsorption spectra after the addition of excess $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ even longer than 24 hours (Fig. S7). Similarly, almost no reaction was found in a control experiment in the presence of pure UiO-66 particles (Fig. S8). In contrast, when a trace amount of UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles were added into the reaction mixture (Fig. 7b), the absorbance at about $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ decreased quickly and a new peak at about 300 nm appeared and intensified gradually, which was due to the production of 4- aminophenol (4-AP).[35] The reaction was finished in $1.5\ \mathrm{min}$ and the solution became colorless when the concentration of Pd NPs $(C_{\mathrm{Pd}})$ was $2\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{mM}$ . This phenomenon indicated that the UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles exhibited good catalytic activity. The catalytic mechanism might be related to the electron transfer involving the reactant molecules, Pd NPs and PPy (Scheme 2). On one hand, electron transfer took place from $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ to 4-NP through adsorption of both reactant molecules onto the surface of Pd NPs.[33, 36] On the other hand, the electrons could be transferred from PPy to Pd NPs, which led to an electron-enriched region, because the PPy $(5.0\ \mathrm{eV})$ had a lower work function than Pd NPs ( $5.12\ \mathrm{eV})$ . The surplus electrons inside the Pd NPs also facilitated the uptake of electrons by 4-NP when it happened to be close to these electronenriched region.[37, 38] In Fig. S9 and Fig. S10, the two control experiments using Pd
NPs or the mixture of UiO-66 particles and Pd NPs as catalyst further demonstrated that the Pd NPs played the role of catalyst in the reduction of 4-NP by $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . Since $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ was added in large excess in our study, the reaction process could be assumed as a pseudo-first-order reaction. Reasonably, a linear relationship between $\ln(C_{t}/C_{0})$ and the reaction time $t$ was represented in Fig. 7c (where $C_{t}$ and $C_{0}$ was assigned to the concentration of nitrophenolate ion at time $t$ and 0, respectively), which was well matching with first-order reaction kinetics. The apparent reaction constant $k_{\mathrm{app}}$ was calculated to be $2.86\times10^{-2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . Further investigations were performed using different concentrations of catalyst particles (Fig. S11 and Fig. S12). Notably, the values of $k_{\mathrm{app}}$ were found to be proportional to $C_{\mathrm{Pd}}$ , as illustrated in Fig. S13. It indicated the catalytic activity of UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles was unrelated to their concentration, but directly and proportionally influencing their catalytic efficiency.
Therefore, the $k_{\mathrm{nor}}$ defined by normalizing $k_{\mathrm{app}}$ to $C_{\mathrm{Pd}}$ would offer a reasonable comparison with similar study reported by others. It can be seen from Table 1, the $k_{\mathrm{nor}}$ of our UiO$66/\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{PPy}$ nanocomposite particles is about $\widehat{14.30\mathrm{\s}^{-1}\mathrm{\mM}^{-1}}$ , superior to the value in most of previous reports. The excellent catalytic activity of our catalyst particles in this work could be attributed to the following reasons.[39-42] First, the porous structure of UiO-66 particles and the nanocomposite shell are beneficial for the adsorption of 4-NP because of п-п electron interactions, thus enriching 4-NP around the catalyst particles. Secondly, the highly dispersed and small Pd NPs provide more active sites for the catalysis and at the same time the PPy contribute to enhancing the stability of Pd NPs during the catalytic process. Finally, the combination of Pd NPs and PPy can promote the electron transfer between 4-NP and Pd NPs and therefore remarkably improve the catalytic activity of Pd NPs.
In addition to the high catalytic activity, the stability of catalyst particles is also an important characteristic in their practical applications. Four successive cycles were performed and at the end of each cycle, the UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles were separated out from the previous reaction system by centrifugation and reused in a fresh reaction solution. As depicted in Fig. 7d, the reaction rate decreases slightly in the four cycles, but the reduction reaction can still be completed in 4 min. Meanwhile, the conversion (more than $90\%$ ) of 4-AP remains almost unchanged even after four cycles of the reaction. As a result, the UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles can be used as an efficient heterogeneous catalyst with good stability, thanks to the unique protective effect of PPy for Pd NPs.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, the functional nanocomposite shell composed of palladium nanoparticles (Pd NPs) and polypyrrole (PPy) was successfully coated on the surface of UiO-66 particle for the first time by a double solvents method (abbreviated as UiO-66/Pd $@$ PPy nanocomposite particles). The synthetic approach has several remarkable advantages, including: 1) greatly simplifying synthetic procedure for such a multicomponent nanostructure because of being free of any surface pretreatments of UiO-66 particles; 2) judiciously selecting the combination of pyrrole and $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ resulting in a simple one-pot preparation of $\operatorname{Pd}\ @\operatorname{PPy}$ nanocomposite coating on the
MOF support; 3) easily tuning the morphology and composition of nanocomposite shell containing PPy and Pd NPs by changing the weight ratio of the UiO-66 particles and pyrrole. Furthermore, in addition to its simplicity and controllability, our proposed synthetic strategy is rather versatile and can be used for the fabrication of UiO-66/Au $@$ PPy and UiO-66/Ag@PPy nanocomposite particles. More significantly, the resultant UiO-66/Pd@PPy nanocomposite particles showed high catalytic activity and stability in the reduction of $p$ -nitrophenol by NaBH4. Our strategy reported in this work affords a new methodology to functionalize MOF particles with various coatings composed of conducting polymers and noble metal NPs, and allows us to apply different nanocomposite materials by meeting the needs of diverse applications.
